<StoryLayout story={story}>
  <div class="container">
    <section class="section" id="s01">
      <h2><span class="num">01 — Context</span>The Substrate</h2>
      <p>The files did not describe an AI model. They described the machine that builds one — the architecture of Google's custom tensor processing unit chips, the cluster management software that orchestrates thousands of them into a single supercomputer, the specialized network interface cards that shuttle data between them at speeds no commercial hardware can match. Over the course of a year, a Google software engineer named Linwei Ding copied more than a thousand of these files to a personal cloud account. He was convicted of espionage. Then a federal judge found that the evidence could not support the charge. Ding remains convicted on seven felony counts of trade-secret theft and awaits sentencing.</p>
      <p>What prosecutors described as seven categories of trade secrets covered the full stack of how Google designs, builds, and operates its AI data centers. TPU chip architecture, including low-level instruction sets, performance characteristics tied to high-bandwidth memory access, and inter-chip connectivity. GPU cluster orchestration — how Google configures and runs multi-GPU systems at scale. The software that allows chips to communicate and execute tasks. A Cluster Management System that schedules workloads across thousands of processors. A custom-designed SmartNIC for high-bandwidth, low-latency networking inside AI clusters. The hardware infrastructure and the software platform that ties it together. More than a thousand unique files. Over two thousand pages of proprietary documents. Of these, 105 formed the core of the criminal case.</p>
      <p>This was not training data. It was not model weights. It was the substrate — the physical and software infrastructure without which large-scale AI training does not happen. Google has spent billions of dollars and more than a decade developing these systems. The files described how to replicate them.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="phantom-wrap">
      <canvas class="google-ding-ai-trade-secrets-canvas" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
    </div>
    <p class="canvas-note"><strong>The Clause</strong> — Two identical particle lattices float side by side; one reaches a thin tether toward a distant node that never responds -- the tether snaps red, the reaching lattice quietly scatters, and the untethered lattice persists alone.</p>
    <section class="section" id="s02">
      <h2><span class="num">02 — What Happened</span>The Taking</h2>
      <p>Linwei Ding joined Google in May 2019 as a software engineer working on supercomputing data centers for AI model training. The exfiltration method was not sophisticated. Ding copied data from Google's internal network into the Apple Notes application on his Google-issued MacBook laptop, converted the notes to PDF files, and uploaded the PDFs to a personal Google Cloud account. Google's data loss prevention systems did not flag the transfer. The intermediary step — Apple Notes — bypassed the detection layer.</p>
      <div class="gd-exfil-wrap">
        <div class="gd-exfil-chain">
          <div class="gd-exfil-node">
            <div class="gd-exfil-node-label">Google Internal Network</div>
            <div class="gd-exfil-node-sub">Proprietary files</div>
          </div>
          <div class="gd-exfil-arrow">
            <div class="gd-exfil-arrow-label">&nbsp;</div>
            <div class="gd-exfil-arrow-line"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="gd-exfil-node">
            <div class="gd-exfil-node-label">Apple Notes</div>
            <div class="gd-exfil-node-sub">Google-issued MacBook</div>
          </div>
          <div class="gd-exfil-arrow">
            <div class="gd-exfil-arrow-label">Copy to PDF</div>
            <div class="gd-exfil-arrow-line"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="gd-exfil-node">
            <div class="gd-exfil-node-label">PDF Files</div>
            <div class="gd-exfil-node-sub">Local storage</div>
          </div>
          <div class="gd-exfil-arrow">
            <div class="gd-exfil-arrow-label">Upload</div>
            <div class="gd-exfil-arrow-line"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="gd-exfil-node gd-exfil-endpoint">
            <div class="gd-exfil-node-label">Personal Google Cloud</div>
            <div class="gd-exfil-node-sub">External account</div>
          </div>
          <div class="gd-exfil-dlp-wrap">
            <div class="gd-exfil-dlp">
              <div class="gd-exfil-dlp-label">Google DLP Layer</div>
            </div>
            <div class="gd-exfil-bypass">Bypass</div>
            <div class="gd-exfil-dlp"></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="gd-exfil-context">The data loss prevention system did not extend to Apple Notes. The gap was mundane.</div>
      </div>
      <p>The uploading began around May 21, 2022. By approximately June 2022, Ding entered discussions to become chief technology officer of Beijing Rongshu Lianzhi Technology Co., Ltd., a private early-stage technology company in China focused on machine learning acceleration. During an extended trip to China from October 2022 through March 2023, he took the CTO role, raised capital for the company, and accumulated twenty percent of its stock. The position paid 100,000 RMB per month — approximately $14,800. While in China, he arranged for a colleague at Google's Mountain View office to regularly scan his employee badge, creating the appearance that he was working in the United States.</p>
      <p>On May 30, 2023, Ding founded a second company, Shanghai Zhisuan Technology Co., Ltd., and served as its CEO. Shanghai Zhisuan focused on training large AI models using supercomputing chips. The company was accepted into MiraclePlus, a Beijing-based startup accelerator. In November 2023, Ding traveled to Beijing to pitch investors at MiraclePlus Demo Day. In a WeChat message, he stated that he had "experience with Google's ten-thousand-card computational power platform" and planned to "replicate and upgrade it."</p>
      <p>Both Beijing Rongshu and Shanghai Zhisuan were private companies. MiraclePlus is a private accelerator. No source reviewed for this story connects any of these entities to the Chinese government. According to MLex reporter Amy Miller, Judge Vince Chhabria wrote in his August 20, 2026 order that "there was no indication at trial that any of Ding's colleagues in China were affiliated with the government."</p>
      <p>On December 2, 2023, Google discovered suspicious activity — Ding had uploaded additional files while in China. On December 26, 2023, Ding resigned from Google and booked a one-way flight to Beijing. The FBI executed search warrants at his Newark, California home on January 6 and January 13, 2024. On March 6, 2024, Ding was arrested at his home. The original indictment, filed the previous day, charged four counts of trade-secret theft under 18 U.S.C. section 1832. It did not include espionage charges.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="s03">
      <h2><span class="num">03 — The Mechanism</span>The Statute</h2>
      <p>The espionage charges came later. On February 4, 2025, a federal grand jury returned a superseding indictment expanding the case from four counts to fourteen: seven counts of economic espionage under 18 U.S.C. section 1831 and seven counts of theft of trade secrets under 18 U.S.C. section 1832. The two statutes sit side by side in the Economic Espionage Act of 1996. They prohibit the same conduct. They differ on a single element.</p>
      <p>Both statutes cover the same acts: stealing, copying, uploading, transmitting, or conveying a trade secret. The prohibited conduct is substantively identical. The difference is the intent each statute requires. Section 1831 — economic espionage — requires that the defendant act "intending or knowing that the offense will benefit any foreign government, foreign instrumentality, or foreign agent." Section 1832 — theft of trade secrets — requires only "intent to convert a trade secret... to the economic benefit of <strong>anyone other than the owner</strong> thereof."</p>
      <div class="gd-statute-wrap">
        <div class="gd-statute-header">
          <div class="gd-statute-header-cell">
            <div class="gd-statute-header-num">18 U.S.C. Section 1831</div>
            <div class="gd-statute-header-label">Economic Espionage</div>
          </div>
          <div class="gd-statute-header-cell">
            <div class="gd-statute-header-num">18 U.S.C. Section 1832</div>
            <div class="gd-statute-header-label">Theft of Trade Secrets</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="gd-statute-shared">
          <div class="gd-statute-shared-label">Shared prohibited conduct</div>
          <div class="gd-statute-shared-text">"Whoever... knowingly -- (1) steals, or without authorization appropriates, takes, carries away, or conceals, or by fraud, artifice, or deception obtains a trade secret; (2) without authorization copies, duplicates... uploads... transmits... or conveys a trade secret..."</div>
        </div>
        <div class="gd-statute-cols">
          <div class="gd-statute-col">
            <div class="gd-statute-col-label">Differentiating element</div>
            <div class="gd-statute-highlight">"intending or knowing that the offense will benefit any foreign government, foreign instrumentality, or foreign agent"</div>
          </div>
          <div class="gd-statute-col">
            <div class="gd-statute-col-label">Differentiating element</div>
            <div class="gd-statute-standard">"with intent to convert a trade secret... to the economic benefit of <strong>anyone other than the owner</strong> thereof"</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="gd-statute-penalty">
          <div class="gd-statute-penalty-cell">Up to <strong>15 years</strong> / <strong>$5,000,000</strong></div>
          <div class="gd-statute-penalty-cell">Up to <strong>10 years</strong> / <strong>$250,000</strong></div>
        </div>
        <div class="gd-statute-status">
          <div class="gd-statute-status-cell">Counts 1--7: Vacated</div>
          <div class="gd-statute-status-cell">Counts 8--14: Guilty</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>One clause. That is the distance between a national-security offense carrying fifteen years and five million dollars in fines, and a commercial offense carrying ten years and two hundred fifty thousand. The prosecution charged both because it could: the same seven sets of files, the same defendant, the same acts. Proving section 1831 required proving one additional thing — that a foreign state stood to benefit.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="s04">
      <h2><span class="num">04 — Consequence</span>The Prosecution</h2>
      <p>When Ding was arrested in March 2024, Attorney General Merrick Garland said, "The Justice Department will not tolerate the theft of artificial intelligence and other advanced technologies that could put our national security at risk."</p>
      <p>The national-security framing intensified at every stage. After the superseding indictment added the espionage counts, prosecutors described the case in terms of state-level threat. After the jury convicted Ding on all fourteen counts on January 29, 2026 — unanimously, after approximately three hours of deliberation, following an eleven-day trial — the Department of Justice presented the verdict as a national-security victory. John A. Eisenberg, Assistant Attorney General for National Security, called it "a calculated breach of trust involving some of the most advanced AI technology in the world." He said Ding had been "pursuing PRC government-aligned ventures" and that "[h]is duplicity put U.S. technological leadership and competitiveness at risk." Sanjay Virmani, the FBI's San Francisco Special Agent in Charge, said the case involved "the theft and misuse of advanced artificial intelligence technology for the benefit of the People's Republic of China." DOJ characterized the verdict as the first conviction on AI-related economic espionage charges.</p>
      <p>The court would subsequently find — as Reuters and MLex reported — insufficient evidence for the very element that this rhetoric depended on: foreign-government benefit.</p>
      <p>Defense counsel Grant Fondo of Goodwin Procter said after the verdict, "We respect the jury's verdict, but we are obviously disappointed." Google's VP of Regulatory Affairs, Lee-Anne Mulholland, said the company was "grateful to the jury for making sure justice was served today."</p>
      <p>Three weeks after the verdict, Ding's defense team filed a motion under Federal Rule of Criminal Procedure 29(c), seeking a post-verdict judgment of acquittal on the espionage counts. In the alternative, they sought a new trial under Rule 33. The motion argued that the evidence presented at trial had never been sufficient to prove the foreign-government-benefit element of section 1831. Six months later, the trial judge agreed.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="s05">
      <h2><span class="num">05 — What Changed</span>The Ruling</h2>
      <p>On August 20, 2026, Judge Vince Chhabria filed Docket #447 in United States v. Linwei Ding: an order granting the defense motion for judgment of acquittal on all seven economic espionage counts under 18 U.S.C. section 1831. The seven trade-secret-theft convictions under section 1832 were upheld.</p>
      <p>The procedural mechanism matters. A post-verdict judgment of acquittal under Federal Rule of Criminal Procedure 29(c) is not a reversal on appeal. It is not a new trial. It is not a dismissal. It is the trial court finding, as a matter of law, that the evidence was insufficient to sustain the conviction on those counts. The acquittal on the section 1831 counts is final. The guilty verdicts on the section 1832 counts stand.</p>
      <p>According to MLex reporter Amy Miller, Judge Chhabria described the Ding prosecution as "the rare criminal trial where the government managed to obtain a conviction despite lacking sufficient evidence to prove an element beyond a reasonable doubt." The element in question was the foreign-government-benefit clause of section 1831.</p>
      <p>In a passage reported by MLex, Chhabria wrote: "There was no indication at trial that any of Ding's colleagues in China were affiliated with the government. Nor was there indication that he intended to actually provide trade secrets to anyone in the Chinese government. The evidence merely suggested that, by that September-December 2023 period, he developed an intent to make money off the Chinese government."</p>
      <p>The distinction is precise. The judge found some evidence that Ding, in the final months of the charged period, had developed a financial interest in Chinese government contracts — an intent to profit from the government as a customer. That is not the same as intending to benefit the government, which is what section 1831 requires. The statute's element asks whether the defendant meant for the foreign state to gain from the offense. The evidence, as characterized in wire reporting, showed commercial ambition directed at a government market, not intent to serve a foreign state's interests.</p>
      <p>The full text of Judge Chhabria's order was not publicly available at the time of this article's publication. All characterizations of the court's reasoning are based on reporting by Reuters (Tom Hals) and MLex (Amy Miller).</p>
