        <div class="gd-verdict-context">Same defendant. Same files. Same jury. Same trial. Different statute. Different result.</div>
      </div>
      <p>An attorney for Ding told Reuters he was "gratified with the ruling." The Department of Justice did not respond to requests for comment. Ding is scheduled to be sentenced on September 1, 2026, according to Reuters' reading of the court docket, on the seven surviving trade-secret-theft convictions.</p>
      <p>The government can, as a legal matter, appeal a post-verdict Rule 29(c) judgment of acquittal when the jury returned a guilty verdict — because reinstating the conviction would not require a new trial and does not offend the Double Jeopardy Clause. Whether DOJ will seek such an appeal is unknown as of this article's publication.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="s06">
      <h2><span class="num">06 — Signal</span>The Pattern</h2>
      <p>This is not the first time the foreign-government-benefit element of section 1831 has broken a prosecution. In 2012, Judge Ruben Castillo of the Northern District of Illinois acquitted Hanjuan Jin of economic espionage charges after finding that prosecutors failed to prove her theft of Motorola trade secrets was for the benefit of the Chinese military. Jin was convicted on three counts of trade-secret theft under section 1832 and sentenced to four years in prison. The Seventh Circuit affirmed.</p>
      <p>The technology was different — Motorola's iDEN mobile-radio platform, not AI supercomputing infrastructure. The defendant was different. The alleged foreign beneficiary was different (the Chinese military, not the Chinese government writ large). The outcome was the same. The same statutory element, requiring proof that a foreign state stood behind the theft, failed against the same evidentiary standard. Fourteen years apart, the pattern repeated.</p>
      <p>Section 1831 convictions remain rare, in part because the foreign-government-benefit element is difficult to prove. The statute was written in 1996, when the paradigmatic trade-secret threat was a state intelligence service directing an operative to extract secrets from a defense contractor. The element made sense in that world. The beneficiary was a government. The chain of direction was discoverable.</p>
      <p>The AI infrastructure landscape does not match that geometry. The actors are private companies funded by venture capital, staffed by commercial engineers, incorporated under commercial law. A software engineer who copies files to start a competing company is committing theft. Whether a foreign government benefits from that theft is a separate question — one that prosecutors in both the Ding and Jin cases could not answer to the satisfaction of a federal judge.</p>
      <p>Ding remains convicted on seven felony counts of trade-secret theft under 18 U.S.C. section 1832. He awaits sentencing. The espionage charge that would have classified this case as a national-security crime required proving a foreign state stood behind the taking. The court found — according to wire reporting by Reuters and MLex — that the evidence did not reach that bar. The question the case leaves behind is whether the law's highest tier for protecting strategic technology can reach the cases where the technology is most strategic, when the actors transferring it are private companies operating in a competitive landscape the statute was not written to describe.</p>
      <p>—-</p>
    </section>
